\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_ddr_trajectory.pdf}
\caption{Feature trajectories across generation depth. \textbf{Top row (drifting)}: entropy mean and hapax ratio decline monotonically across all eight generators ($|\rho| > 0.59$). \textbf{Bottom row (stationary)}: entropy max and tail decay remain flat ($|\rho| < 0.07$). Colored lines: individual generators; diamonds: cross-generator mean (blue for drifting, amber for stationary). This dichotomy defines the differential depth response (DDR).}
\label{fig:ddr-trajectory}
\end{figure*}

Not all distributional properties respond equally to recursive generation.
Figure~\ref{fig:ddr-trajectory} reveals a striking dichotomy that holds across all eight generators: central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) decline monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay, entropy min) remain flat.
We term this asymmetry the \emph{differential depth response} (DDR).
The primary partition is binary: $k{=}2$ clustering in standardized $(|\rho|, d)$ space yields silhouette\,=\,0.660 (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}), cleanly separating three \emph{drifting} features ($|\rho| > 0.60$, $d{=}0.76$--$1.17$) from four \emph{non-drifting} ones ($d \leq 0.55$); higher $k$ degrades cluster quality ($k{=}3$: silhouette\,=\,0.532).
A domain-informed refinement resolves entropy std as a sole \emph{intermediate} member ($d{=}0.55$, $|\rho|{=}0.16$), distinct from the three near-stationary features ($d < 0.10$; CV shift $<$3\% vs.\ $>$15\% for drifting features).
Five complementary effect-size measures converge on this drifting/non-drifting partition, confirming that the location/shape asymmetry reflects distributional structure rather than feature-selection artifact.
This partition is further validated by generator-split consistency analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}), where the drifting/near-stationary boundary persists across all eight hold-out splits.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response (DDR). Diamonds: cross-generator means; dots: per-generator values. Drifting features (blue; $|\rho| > 0.60$, $d{=}0.76$--$1.17$) show strong monotonic depth sensitivity; non-drifting features remain stable. Entropy std (wine; $d{=}0.55$) is an intermediate member; three near-stationary features (amber; $d < 0.10$) show negligible drift. Entropy min ($|\rho|{=}0.40$ pooled) is a boundary feature whose sensitivity concentrates at the gen-0/gen-1 transition.}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled class-balanced Spearman correlations with depth confirm the binary split (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}):
drifting features show substantial monotonic trends (hapax ratio $\rho = -0.64$, TTR $\rho = -0.63$, entropy mean $\rho = -0.60$),
while non-drifting features remain near-zero (tail decay $|\rho| = 0.04$; per-depth distributions in Appendix~\ref{app:feature-distributions}).
The six-fold magnitude gap between drifting ($|\rho| > 0.60$) and non-drifting features ($|\rho| \leq 0.16$) defines the DDR partition.
Within the non-drifting group, entropy std ($|\rho| = 0.16$, $d{=}0.55$) shows a substantial level shift despite low monotonicity, distinguishing it from the three near-stationary features ($d < 0.10$); this refinement is stable across $d$ thresholds in $[0.56, 0.75]$ (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).

\paragraph{Quantification.}
Every tested effect-size metric confirms the split.
Three parametric measures (Cohen's $d$, Glass's $\Delta$, KS statistic) primarily capture location shift; Cliff's $\delta$ provides a rank-based perspective, and CV shift captures relative variability changes.
Cohen's $d$ \cite{cohen1988statistical} converges with $\rho$: drifting features show large effects ($d{=}0.76$--$1.17$) while shape features are near zero ($d \leq 0.08$); KS statistics show a ${\sim}10\times$ gap (0.516--0.537 vs.\ 0.043--0.052; all $p \approx 0$).
Glass's $\Delta$ and Cliff's $\delta$ corroborate the partition with a ${\geq}9.5\times$ gap; CV shift ($<$3\% vs.\ $>$15\%) supports ``near-stationary'' as a practical characterization (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).
Five features fall cleanly into drifting or non-drifting groups; the remaining two require qualification.
Entropy min is a \emph{boundary feature}: its gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+ effect is near-stationary ($d{=}0.016$) despite a substantial pooled $|\rho|$ (0.40) driven by the gen-0/gen-1 transition (Cliff's $\delta{=}0.354$ confirms shape change without location shift); $k{=}2$ clustering places it with non-drifting features (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).
Entropy std shows a substantial level shift ($d{=}0.55$) despite low monotonicity ($|\rho|{=}0.16$), making it the sole intermediate feature.
\paragraph{Validation.}
The partition is not a pooling artifact.
Per-generator TOST equivalence testing ($\varepsilon{=}|d|{<}0.20$, following Cohen's~\citeyearpar{cohen1988statistical} conventional threshold for small effects; gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) confirms stability for entropy min (8/8 generators pass) and entropy max (6/8); tail decay passes for only 4/8 generators, with OLMo-2-1B ($d{=}0.22$) exceeding the equivalence bound (partition stability across $\varepsilon \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$ is reported in Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).
Bootstrap resampling (1{,}000 iterations) on the pooled sample achieves 100\% rejection for all three near-stationary features, but high pooled power ($N \approx 3{,}500$--$4{,}500$ per generator) can mask per-generator heterogeneity.
A permutation test ($n{=}10{,}000$) confirms the observed three-vs-four split is non-random ($p{=}0.030$; fixed-label silhouette\,=\,0.660 vs.\ null mean\,=\,0.46); exhaustive enumeration of all $\binom{7}{3}{=}35$ possible three-vs-four splits confirms this partition achieves the highest silhouette (rank 1/35; Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).
Tail decay occupies a boundary position: 4 of 8 generators pass TOST at $\varepsilon{=}0.20$, and its per-generator effect size ($|d|{=}0.10$--$0.22$) spans the intermediate-to-near-stationary range.
We retain it in the near-stationary group for parsimony, but note that reclassifying it as intermediate (3/2/2 partition) preserves the core drifting/non-drifting split and leaves classification performance unchanged, since the classifier uses all seven features regardless of the interpretive partition.

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773) outperforms surface-only (0.688) and reference-only (0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table}).
PCA confirms the three drifting features share a single latent dimension (92.3\% variance; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}); replacing them with PC1 costs only 1.1\,pp, confirming that DDR's contribution is identifying \emph{which} distributional properties drift, not classification per se.
TTR and hapax ratio ($r = 0.97$) are both retained for marginal LOFO gains despite feature masking \cite{strobl_conditional_2008} (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) achieve comparable LOGO F1 (spread 0.013; Appendix~\ref{app:cross-ref}), bounding the same-family affinity effect; depth features are robust to reference model choice.

\paragraph{Temperature and prompt-type sensitivity.}
All four tested generators degrade at $t{=}0.9$ ($-9.3$ to $-26.0$\,pp binary F1); low-temperature improvement is family-dependent ($+7$--$9$\,pp for Pythia/OLMo at $t{=}0.5$, negligible for Qwen; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}).
Continuation chains show the strongest drift (F1\,=\,0.781), with cross-prompt-type transfer confirming shared signal (LOPTO F1\,=\,0.753; Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}); the DDR partition hierarchy is preserved across temperatures despite absolute effect-size compression (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-temp-stability}).


\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

Existing detection methods target binary real-vs-synthetic classification; their continuous scores encode distributional divergence that may incidentally correlate with recursive depth.
To test this, we repurpose four detection scores as ordinal depth predictors under identical XGBoost and LOGO conditions (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
\midrule
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & --- \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & --- \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & --- \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation; slightly higher than per-generator macro average in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}). Identical XGBoost configuration. 7-feature: one 410M model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles: two models, 1.4B params (2{,}868\,MB). Individual detection scores (N/A) share the reference-model forward pass; per-method timing is not separately profiled.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 vs.\ inference cost (8-gen pooled). The 7-feature ensemble matches multi-detector ensembles at 2.4$\times$ lower cost; individual detectors vary widely when repurposed for depth.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

The 7-feature ensemble surpasses all individual baselines in 3-class F1 ($\Delta$\,=\,6.7--36.3\,pp; Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}) and the pure 4-detector ensemble in binary F1 (0.705 vs.\ 0.700).
Adding DDR's surface features to the four detectors yields the highest binary F1 (0.708), marginally above the 7-feature set at 2.4$\times$ the inference cost.
This result underscores that DDR's primary contribution is not classification performance---where multi-detector ensembles match or marginally exceed it---but the decomposition of depth signal into location-sensitive and shape-stable components, a structural distinction absent from both binary detection methods and model collapse analyses (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Recursive generation leaves a systematic distributional signature: central-tendency features drift monotonically with depth while shape features remain an order of magnitude more stable---a dichotomy we formalize as differential depth response (DDR), validated across eight generators from five model families.
This decomposition enables cross-generator depth classification (leak-free binary F1\,=\,0.694; end-to-end: 0.905) and provides an analytical lens complementary to model collapse: fixed-parameter sampling compresses distributional location while preserving shape, whereas retraining erodes both.
